The dashboard looks great! But I notice a bug: the mobile bottom navigation bar is appearing on desktop! Look at the bottom of the screenshot — there's a bar with "Intake", "Caregivers", "Matching", "Pricing" icons overlapping the content.

This is because `.show-mobile` uses `display: block !important` in the media query, but the base `.show-mobile { display: none; }` — wait let me re-check.

In styles.scss:
```scss.hide-mobile { display: block; }
.show-mobile { display: none; }

@media (max-width:767px) {
 .hide-mobile { display: none !important; }
 .show-mobile { display: block !important; }
}
```

So on desktop (>767px), `.show-mobile` should be `display: none`.But the bottom nav is a `<nav class="bottom-nav show-mobile">`. On desktop it should be hidden. But in the screenshot at1440px wide, it's showing!

Wait — the screenshot is1080px wide (scaled). The viewport was1440x900. Hmm, the screenshot shows the full page at some scale.

Actually looking again, the bottom nav appears. So `.show-mobile` isn't hiding it on desktop.

Why? Because in `app.scss` I have:
```scss.bottom-nav {
 position: fixed;
 bottom:0;
 ...
 display: flex;
 ...
}
```

The `.bottom-nav` rule sets `display: flex`, which OVERRIDES the `.show-mobile { display: none }` because... hmm, specificity. `.show-mobile` is a single class (specificity0,1,0). `.bottom-nav` is also a single class (0,1,0). Same specificity — so the LAST one in the CSS wins.

The component styles (app.scss) are loaded... In Angular, component styles are injected after global styles usually. So `.bottom-nav { display: flex }` from app.scss would override `.show-mobile { display: none }` from styles.scss.

That's the bug. I need to fix it.

Solution: Instead of relying on `.show-mobile` for the bottom nav, I should use a media query directly on `.bottom-nav`:

```scss.bottom-nav {
 display: none;
}

@media (max-width:1023px) {
 .bottom-nav {
 display: flex;
 }
}
```

That's cleaner. Let me fix the app.scss.

